<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brecknockshire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Breconshire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brecknockshire, or Breconshire, is a historic county of south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> with its county town at Brecon. It was created in 1536 from the Norman marcher lordship of Brecknock, itself carved out of the Welsh kingdom of Brycheiniog after 1093. Largely mountainous, it includes much of the Brecon Beacons. Since 1974 it has formed part of Powys.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brecknockshire.jpg" alt="Brecknockshire" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DankJae · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wales_Historic_Counties_map_Brecknockshire.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The county was bounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radnorshire">Radnorshire</a> to the north, Herefordshire and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monmouthshire">Monmouthshire</a> to the east, Monmouthshire and Glamorgan to the south, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiganshire">Cardiganshire</a> to the west. The Black Mountains rise in the south-east, the Brecon Beacons in the centre, Fforest Fawr in the south-west and Mynydd Epynt in the north; the highest point is Pen y Fan, at 886 metres. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-wye">River Wye</a> follows nearly all of the northern boundary, and the Usk flows east through the central valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The origins of Brycheiniog are obscure. Legend derives its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brychan">Brychan</a>, a 5th-century ruler for whom there is no early evidence, and the commemorative stones of the period show an elite but no single dominant leader. A kingdom clearly emerged only in the 9th century and was later absorbed by Deheubarth. The Norman lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-de-neufmarche">Bernard de Neufmarché</a> conquered it and founded the Lordship of Brecknock, while the cantref of Buellt, long ruled by its own dynasty, was taken by Philip de Braose around 1095 and overrun by Edward I in November 1282.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-stafford-3rd-duke-of-buckingham">Edward Stafford, 3rd Duke of Buckingham</a>, was executed for treason, his lordship of Brecknock passed to the crown, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> joined it with Buellt. The Laws in Wales Act 1535 made the combined lordships, towns and commotes into a county with Brecon as its county town, and in 1542 it was divided into six hundreds: Builth, Crickhowell, Devynnock, Merthyr, Penkelly and Talgarth. The borough and the county each returned one member of parliament.</p><p>An elected county council was created in 1889 and met at the Shire Hall in Brecon. The magistrates held that Brecknock was the county's correct name, while the council chose Breconshire by 21 votes to 16, and the two bodies never agreed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under the Local Government Act 1972 the bulk of the county passed in 1974 to Powys as the Brecknock District, while Penderyn, Vaynor, Brynmawr and Llanelly went to neighbouring districts. The Brecknockshire Agricultural Society, founded in 1755, is the oldest continuous society of its kind in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, and Hay-on-Wye, with its bookshops and literary festival, is known as the Town of Books.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brecknockshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1093 the Norman Bernard de Neufmarché defeated and killed Rhys ap Tewdwr near Brecon and created the Norman lordship of Brecknock, which later became the county of <b>Brecknockshire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-brycheiniog">Kingdom of Brycheiniog</a></li></ul></section>
